---
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import detailScreenshot from "../../../assets/incidetective/incidetective-detail.png";
import listScreenshot from "../../../assets/incidetective/incidetective-list.png";

const pipeline = [
  {
    step: "Import",
    text: "One importer reads most log formats: JSON lines (Pino, Docker, journalctl), key=value logs (slog, logrus), syslog, nginx and Apache access logs, and timestamped text such as log4j. Logs are normalized and stored in PostgreSQL.",
  },
  {
    step: "Cluster",
    text: "Recurring errors are grouped with the Drain algorithm, keeping meaningful error codes intact, and ranked by how often they occur and when.",
  },
  {
    step: "Add context",
    text: "Warnings that came just before each error are pulled in, since the cause often shows up before the failure does.",
  },
  {
    step: "Investigate",
    text: "A local model running on Ollama receives that evidence, not the raw logs, and proposes possible causes with concrete diagnostic steps.",
  },
];

const stack = [
  { area: "Backend", tech: "Node.js, TypeScript, Fastify" },
  { area: "Frontend", tech: "React, Vite, Tailwind CSS, shadcn/ui" },
  { area: "Database", tech: "PostgreSQL, Drizzle ORM" },
  { area: "Log clustering", tech: "Drain" },
  { area: "Local AI", tech: "Ollama, Qwen3" },
  { area: "Testing", tech: "Vitest" },
  { area: "Deployment", tech: "Docker Compose, self-hosted on Coolify" },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Incidetective | Michael"
  description="A self-hosted tool for investigating backend failures, combining deterministic log analysis with a local LLM."
>
  <article class="project-detail">
    <header class="project-detail-header">
      <a href="/projects/" class="back-link">
        ← Back to projects
      </a>

      <p class="eyebrow">Personal project · Working MVP</p>

      <h1>Incidetective</h1>

      <p class="project-lead">
        A self-hosted tool for investigating backend failures. It finds the
        recurring errors in your logs, then uses a local LLM to suggest what
        might have caused them, with every claim pointing back to the log lines
        behind it.
      </p>

      <div class="project-detail-links">
        <a
          href="https://github.com/Ohshi60/Incidetective"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
        >
          View source on GitHub ↗
        </a>
      </div>
    </header>

    <figure class="screenshot screenshot-lead">
      <div class="screenshot-bar" aria-hidden="true">
        <span></span>
        <span></span>
        <span></span>
      </div>
      <Image
        src={detailScreenshot}
        alt="Incidetective incident view: a checkout incident with a summary of 70 errors across 180 logs, and its top failure pattern, a payment gateway timeout seen 40 times, with three original log lines as supporting evidence."
        widths={[800, 1600]}
        sizes="(max-width: 864px) 100vw, 800px"
        loading="eager"
      />
      <figcaption>
        An incident's failure patterns, each backed by the original log lines.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="project-detail-body">
      <section>
        <h2>The problem</h2>
        <p>
          When a service fails, the evidence is usually buried in thousands of
          log lines, often in several formats. Pasting them all into an LLM is
          tempting, but the model then has to find the signal itself. It tends
          to fixate on lines that look alarming but are normal for that system,
          and it's hard to check where its conclusions came from.
        </p>
        <p>
          Incidetective does the mechanical part deterministically first, and
          only then asks a model to reason about the result.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>How it works</h2>
        <ol class="pipeline">
          {pipeline.map((item) => (
            <li>
              <h3>{item.step}</h3>
              <p>{item.text}</p>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ol>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Keeping the AI grounded</h2>
        <p>
          AI output is treated as hypotheses, not answers. A few design choices
          make each one checkable:
        </p>
        <ul class="detail-list">
          <li>
            <strong>Evidence references.</strong> Every hypothesis cites the
            error findings (<code>F1</code>, <code>F2</code>) and contextual
            warnings (<code>C1</code>, <code>C2</code>) it relies on, so you can
            open the original lines and judge for yourself.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Ruled-out evidence.</strong> The model lists
            alarming-looking lines it judged unrelated, with a reason, instead
            of quietly building a theory on them.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Setup notes.</strong> Each service can carry notes on what's
            normal for it: versions, hardware, routine messages. That stops the
            model from blaming things that are expected.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Local models.</strong> Inference runs on Ollama on my own
            hardware, so logs never leave the network.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <figure class="screenshot">
        <div class="screenshot-bar" aria-hidden="true">
          <span></span>
          <span></span>
          <span></span>
        </div>
        <Image
          src={listScreenshot}
          alt="Incidetective incident list with searchable incident cards, each showing its service, severity, title and description."
          widths={[680, 1360]}
          sizes="(max-width: 744px) 100vw, 680px"
        />
        <figcaption>The incident list.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <section>
        <h2>Measuring whether it helps</h2>
        <p>
          The obvious question is whether this beats just pasting the logs into
          the same model. An eval harness answers that. For each incident with a
          known cause, it produces two answers: one from Incidetective's full
          pipeline, and one from the same model given the raw files.
        </p>
        <p>
          The eight cases range from a small synthetic Redis pool exhaustion,
          through Hadoop failures from the Loghub research dataset (up to about
          100,000 lines), to two real incidents from my own machines: a GPU
          backend corrupting Ollama's output, and a Coolify deployment failing
          on a port conflict. Each answer is scored on whether it names the real
          cause, cites the lines that prove it, and suggests a check that would
          confirm it. Scored results go into the pull request that changes the
          analysis, as evidence that the change helped.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Tech stack</h2>
        <dl class="project-facts">
          {stack.map((row) => (
            <div>
              <dt>{row.area}</dt>
              <dd>{row.tech}</dd>
            </div>
          ))}
        </dl>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Limitations and what's next</h2>
        <p>
          It's a working MVP meant for local and trusted environments. Logs are
          imported by hand, AI investigations aren't saved yet, and a model's
          conclusions can still be wrong, which is why the evidence trail
          matters. Next on the roadmap are guided follow-up investigations,
          automated log collection and a public demo mode.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
